{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>For, idols</b> — vanity and falsehood, like 'physicians of no value' [Job 13:4]. It is from the root אל, [in the sense of] 'no' [אל] is in it, or 'no' [אל] is he. And language falls short of expressing it, like 'better than both is he whom the eye has not seen' [i.e., one who has never been]." "<b>And the meaning of heavens</b> — the dwellings of the angels that he mentioned. And it is the fearsome Name upon the souls, for they are noble forms, while He made the bodies."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 96, :notes {0 "The Hebrew of the second clause is compressed and elliptical; rendered hyper-literally. The final comparison is to Ecclesiastes 6:3-5; Ibn Ezra's phrasing is condensed and the argument stays compressed.", 1 "'The fearsome Name' (השם הנורא) is a standard reverent circumlocution for the Tetragrammaton; rendered literally without resolving which divine name is intended."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:37:27.267135559Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}